-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473979}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "62b39803-cf8b-5f2a-91f9-b83f48f12d0e" :question/math-academy-id "q-47919" :question/problem "![](images/d9/d9d8fb6d663d8dfd0cce24ae3817c936e2e97ab2df7aefeaa0c734caf0311b94.png)\n\nThe graph of $y=f(x)$ is plotted above. Given that $f(x)$ is defined on the interval $x\\in [-3,10]$, which points represent the global maximum of $f(x)?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The global extrema of a function are the lowest or highest points of the function's graph over its entire domain.\n\nThe lowest point is the global minimum, whereas the highest point is the global maximum.\n\nAccording to the graph, the highest points are $B$ and $D$, and so these points represents the global maximum of $f(x).$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402929 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129097 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "485fd759-96b3-553f-9eaa-849878c30885" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity Given a Function Definition"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d8e66c6a-76aa-5f0b-b811-79c45ca6df47" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473170} {:answer/id #uuid "dff2787b-869a-5ea4-86c0-502ab98cfeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473171}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dff2787b-869a-5ea4-86c0-502ab98cfeb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473171} :answer-field/id #uuid "97f61a2d-9a0e-548d-95ff-b9e978fe7e11" :answer-field/key "It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473980} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "6300a406-232e-5eef-ae3b-2d4bee23c18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473172} {:answer/id #uuid "e552de5b-1e90-5d9e-b6d7-bcd217ee56a6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473173}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "6300a406-232e-5eef-ae3b-2d4bee23c18e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473172} :answer-field/id #uuid "b3f7ead7-1696-5c6f-b452-9441edd8edd7" :answer-field/key "It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473981} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "dbd6c29c-4ed2-5b06-a0c8-18fdf2489de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473174} {:answer/id #uuid "06ad3490-3bb8-5d66-96d9-9a728de2ffaa" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473175}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "dbd6c29c-4ed2-5b06-a0c8-18fdf2489de6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473174} :answer-field/id #uuid "68758702-d74b-56fc-8a38-738ae43a9997" :answer-field/key "It is continuous at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473982}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "022bd345-534b-5a28-b7c0-bb66d9319957" :question/math-academy-id "q-48144" :question/problem "Which of the following statements are true regarding the function $f(x)$ below?\n\n$$\nf(x)={\\begin{aligned}3x-1, & x\\ne -1 \\\\ 4, & x=-1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For $x=-1,$ let's go through our continuity checklist:\n\n- $f(-1)=4$ exists $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Let's now check the left and right limits. We see that\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4, \\\\ {lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x) & ={lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}(3x-1)=3(-1)-1=-4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, the overall limit exists and ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)=-4.\\,\\,✓$\n\n- Finally, we check that the value of the limit equals the value of the function: \n\n$$\nf(-1)=4\\ne {lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x).\n$$\n\nSo the value of the limit does not equal the value of the function.\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$\n\nHence, “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402936 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ca4417da-4900-5915-99c4-8dbf8511c24e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473176} {:answer/id #uuid "f14717c7-4945-5c40-b6ab-eff1c6903a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473177}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f14717c7-4945-5c40-b6ab-eff1c6903a23" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473177} :answer-field/id #uuid "42c4fde6-2f3d-517c-9d18-84fa25a9eb9d" :answer-field/key "It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473983} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "317e159b-ef08-5a9d-9add-461b6035c10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473178} {:answer/id #uuid "58d88f6e-952b-5ee4-9690-9ae0a991e90d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473179}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "317e159b-ef08-5a9d-9add-461b6035c10e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473178} :answer-field/id #uuid "eee9a05c-c60e-522b-8ea8-1b4e1b1aac3a" :answer-field/key "It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473984} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f023226a-28d4-5239-b6e6-d12b8f7252d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473180} {:answer/id #uuid "31ffd173-df15-56b2-bdd2-8517a204d234" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473181}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f023226a-28d4-5239-b6e6-d12b8f7252d9" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473180} :answer-field/id #uuid "54843998-99e8-583c-bf1f-6a973ea60109" :answer-field/key "It is continuous at $x=-1.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473985}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "825ab862-b738-5389-82d3-382684f52334" :question/math-academy-id "q-48185" :question/problem "![](images/78/786d223be90c13335ab80672631dcbef25297688ae9e318928e744ebbf8a5cdf.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-1)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -1}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-1)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-1$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-1.$” is true, and “It has a removable discontinuity at $x=-1.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-1.$” are false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402943 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186129087 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "07b63c0c-daaa-5f5b-acb3-58d6f36ee869" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying a Jump Discontinuity on a Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c2e891da-5a55-5bf0-a854-65e4e3c2f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473182} {:answer/id #uuid "160ec548-dc6f-50dd-a574-bf19fa0f3e9f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473183}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c2e891da-5a55-5bf0-a854-65e4e3c2f83d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473182} :answer-field/id #uuid "abfcd0b4-d167-5371-9b5c-9bdd172f18bd" :answer-field/key "It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473986} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4d2239bc-88e7-5940-b3cd-8cc6a1b00722" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473184} {:answer/id #uuid "f20f1b28-8e76-5417-8d8c-88c952349f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473185}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f20f1b28-8e76-5417-8d8c-88c952349f34" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473185} :answer-field/id #uuid "a2871067-63fa-5336-983a-e07e554d90ab" :answer-field/key "It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473987} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "42aa8c88-d213-5836-bf0d-bfd3183e6d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473186} {:answer/id #uuid "474b65d1-c2c0-5f81-9774-7f5c35723eb0" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473187}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "42aa8c88-d213-5836-bf0d-bfd3183e6d07" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473186} :answer-field/id #uuid "d4c02913-f997-54e1-8a6e-86e0ac7a51df" :answer-field/key "It is continuous at $x=-2.$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473988}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "ed867e40-29b3-530b-96c3-6f5fbbb12043" :question/math-academy-id "q-48187" :question/problem "![](images/03/034e2813b404338a1b8d0ecfdb509781f7917e356155474d788d8bd85df0f221.png)\n\nWhich of the following statements are true regarding the function $y=f(x)$ plotted above?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "The function has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$ Checking our conditions, we see that\n\n- $f(-2)=2$ exists, $\\,\\,✓$\n\n- ${lim}_{x\\to -2}f(x)$ does not exist $\\,\\,\\times $\n\nSo the second condition failed. It failed because the left and right-sided limits are not equal:\n\n$$\n{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{-}}f(x)=1,\\,\\,{lim}_{x\\to (-2)^{+}}f(x)=2.\n$$\n\nThese limits both exist and are finite, yet they are not equal. Therefore, the discontinuity at $x=-2$ is a jump discontinuity.\n\nConsequently, “It has a jump discontinuity at $x=-2.$” is true, and “It has a point discontinuity at $x=-2.$” and “It is continuous at $x=-2.$” are false."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402964 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f8236aee-487d-5c9a-b3af-96bbad95fbb4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473188} {:answer/id #uuid "90a9cec7-b5d1-5c13-a9b5-12c7a871ca41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473189}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "90a9cec7-b5d1-5c13-a9b5-12c7a871ca41" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473189} :answer-field/id #uuid "ea68800e-b34b-56a9-acea-a35da7ad7623" :answer-field/key "$g(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473989} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ffedeaf-f9c0-5a04-8c4c-2e4ba5ff3b28" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473190} {:answer/id #uuid "07566218-fc9c-5e62-a1bc-0b79ed47046e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473191}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "07566218-fc9c-5e62-a1bc-0b79ed47046e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473191} :answer-field/id #uuid "e53bb7d2-1c6e-5e9a-b1df-91d04df5690c" :answer-field/key "$s(x)=(x-2)^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473990} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "189aff89-6fd7-5e25-9403-8efc112776de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473192} {:answer/id #uuid "780db3d8-d08b-5b7e-9de2-1cb901dc0b0a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473193}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "189aff89-6fd7-5e25-9403-8efc112776de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473192} :answer-field/id #uuid "30f1e3a7-1998-57e8-9ba2-29193f5ff7a8" :answer-field/key "$h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-3}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473991}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "83188ea1-87cd-5ad1-b8c9-6226665589eb" :question/math-academy-id "q-48498" :question/problem "Which of the following functions satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8]?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "For a function to satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,8]$ and differentiable on $(1,8).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}$ is continuous on $[1,8]$ and has a cusp only at $x=0,$ which is outside $[1,8],$ hence it is differentiable on $(1,8).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $s(x)=(x-2)^{2}$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,8]$ and differentiable on $(1,8).$ Therefore, $s(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $h(x)=\\frac{1}{x-3}$ is not defined at $x=3$, so it is not continuous at $x=3$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,8].$ Therefore, $h(x)$ does not satisfiy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions $g(x)=\\sqrt{{x}^{2}}$ and $s(x)=(x-2)^{2}$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,8].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186402971 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186064280 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "5b4b32f9-8362-5c81-a1fe-4457d91ae571" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Functions That Satisfy the Mean Value Theorem"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "cb8614c0-bffa-5670-a76f-75cde40bd457" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473194} {:answer/id #uuid "c7627e99-1650-5dcd-8432-893ec51d740a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473195}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "c7627e99-1650-5dcd-8432-893ec51d740a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473195} :answer-field/id #uuid "8f3eb53d-1757-50de-a23e-ccec360d93e1" :answer-field/key "$g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473992} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "c5545a2e-f0ba-539a-bf10-c17dafb86