m over the interval $[1,3],$ the function must be continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$\n\nLet's check each function individually.\n\n- The function $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ is continuous and differentiable on $(0,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $g(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function  $s(x)=\\frac{1}{x-2}$ is not defined at $x=2$, so it is not continuous at $x=2$, which means it is not continuous on $[1,3].$ Therefore, $s(x)$ does not satisfy the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\n- The function $h(x)=x-2$ is continuous and differentiable on $(-\\infty ,\\infty ).$ In particular, it  is continuous on $[1,3]$ and differentiable on $(1,3).$ Therefore, $h(x)$ satisfies the conditions of the mean value theorem.\n\nTherefore, only functions $g(x)={log}_{2}⁡x$ and $h(x)=x-2$ satisfy the conditions for the mean value theorem over the interval $[1,3].$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403099 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186147592 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "fc7eacaa-2cf2-5cae-a40c-737c98124bcf" :knowledge-point/title "Determining the Degree of a Homogeneous Polynomial Using the Fast Method"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "74716666-cbbe-59ec-85f7-30f9b1e98279" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473200} {:answer/id #uuid "7879e2b4-6c51-54c3-8965-632d19a7a5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473201}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "7879e2b4-6c51-54c3-8965-632d19a7a5e5" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473201} :answer-field/id #uuid "df5134cd-9596-5f7c-9032-9930fdd1fe0e" :answer-field/key "$f(y)=5{y}^{2}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473995} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "33592e60-00fe-5f3f-9e7f-3717aab0ce03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473202} {:answer/id #uuid "2893e195-00a7-55a7-ab3d-901ac20f6329" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473203}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "33592e60-00fe-5f3f-9e7f-3717aab0ce03" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473202} :answer-field/id #uuid "700bce68-d443-5fd4-a8ad-999747fcdae6" :answer-field/key "$g(x,y)=2{x}^{3}y+x{y}^{4}$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473996} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "2cb4acd5-b28e-584e-a4e2-bbe429a6cd45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473204} {:answer/id #uuid "cf74dd46-4fb5-5765-83ec-860c60cbae6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473205}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "cf74dd46-4fb5-5765-83ec-860c60cbae6f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473205} :answer-field/id #uuid "0e047b05-9e5a-5216-9dd8-576728d02a08" :answer-field/key "$h(x,y,z)=x{y}^{2}+y{z}^{2}-xyz$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473997}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "a1f59ac7-fe59-5414-84e5-39e2cb13a6a8" :question/math-academy-id "q-64198" :question/problem "Which of the following polynomials are homogeneous?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "Let's go through each of the options.\n\n- The function $f(y)$ is homogeneous of degree $2.$ It is a polynomial of degree $2,$ with no linear or constant term.\n\n- The function $g(x,y)$ is not homogeneous. It is a polynomial of degree $5.$ However, it also contains a term ($2{x}^{3}y$) that is of degree $4.$ Therefore, not all of its terms are of degree $5.$\n\n- The function $h(x,y,z)$ is homogeneous of degree $3.$ It is a polynomial of degree $3,$ and all of its terms are of degree $3.$"} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403172 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152281 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a34d342c-e7fc-581b-b3d1-ed6a49f1a62f" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying Boundary Value Problems"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "f3605ffc-05b5-51a3-9509-c7162b1bdd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473206} {:answer/id #uuid "e4cdba7d-5e25-502f-9018-56499b97d7bc" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473207}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "f3605ffc-05b5-51a3-9509-c7162b1bdd5c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473206} :answer-field/id #uuid "eeeb8beb-615e-5f3f-8ff7-36520bc8fdb7" :answer-field/key "${y}^{″}+4x{y}^{′}+2y$ $=0,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=-2,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(0)$ $=3$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473998} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "d78562e7-b448-502e-9e5d-132b08f2e202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473208} {:answer/id #uuid "ad3d3eb7-0060-5326-b4e1-e2b3daa6ab8e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473209}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "d78562e7-b448-502e-9e5d-132b08f2e202" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473208} :answer-field/id #uuid "e07ef496-d279-5780-9a25-527eb55b2602" :answer-field/key "$4{y}^{″}+y=0$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473999} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b51141d-8764-5977-a188-03b758754475" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473210} {:answer/id #uuid "786ef073-3f0d-5149-abdc-4edcd620b314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473211}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "786ef073-3f0d-5149-abdc-4edcd620b314" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473211} :answer-field/id #uuid "bac84c97-4cc2-58e5-a587-b2e755fb00d8" :answer-field/key "$x{y}^{″}+2{y}^{′}-y$ $=0,$ $\\,y(0)$ $=-4,$ $\\,{y}^{′}(1)$ $=-4$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474000}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "b765011e-9d19-55a7-93ef-fd380a1d1bd5" :question/math-academy-id "q-65012" :question/problem "Which of the following systems is a boundary value problem?\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "If a second-order differential equation for the function $y(x)$ is given with two conditions specified at *different* locations in the domain of $y,$ then this is a **boundary value problem**.\n\nLet's go through each equation in turn.\n\n- The first system is not a boundary value problem. The differential equation is combined with two conditions, but both conditions are given at the same location in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$). This is an **initial value problem.**\n\n- The second system is not a boundary value problem. It contains an equation only. No conditions are specified.\n\n- The third system is a boundary value problem. It consists of a differential equation together with two conditions at different points in the domain of $y(x)$ (i.e., at $x=0$ and $x=1$)."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403223 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186115222 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "80449399-3ae4-593f-8c3b-00bcdcc99727" :knowledge-point/title "Solving Trigonometric Equations Outside of the Range of Cosecant"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "813d5f30-9e48-5a6a-b6e6-2bb8fba1d9b3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473212} {:answer/id #uuid "e7b647d2-7171-5fbc-aa5a-c488c8e93a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473213}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "e7b647d2-7171-5fbc-aa5a-c488c8e93a1c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473213} :answer-field/id #uuid "3e6082c7-bc86-5235-bb42-ac7ce8892f29" :answer-field/key "$4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474001} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "ed140b0d-d3db-5c2d-ac0b-434ba7495455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473214} {:answer/id #uuid "fe733efc-71ed-5232-84aa-79a588a2cd3f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473215}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "ed140b0d-d3db-5c2d-ac0b-434ba7495455" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473214} :answer-field/id #uuid "23d54bab-920f-59ea-8160-b91090ed180c" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474002} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "48ee1938-d7cd-558b-992d-712e4a9fa218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473216} {:answer/id #uuid "8882b4b9-afef-54ed-bfed-ffed789bf066" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473217}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "48ee1938-d7cd-558b-992d-712e4a9fa218" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186473216} :answer-field/id #uuid "448a5a07-4da4-56f2-ba82-091fe453cab3" :answer-field/key "$\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186474003}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "28fa7313-ebe9-5114-aa43-a48667cbf78a" :question/math-academy-id "q-66005" :question/problem "Which of the following equations has no solutions for $0\\le x<2\\pi ?$\n\nSelect all that apply." :question/worked-solution "We first simplify the given equations, and get\n\n$4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=-\\frac{1}{3}$\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+10=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=10$\n\n$\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=2\\operatorname{csc}⁡x-6$. $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=6$\n\nNow, recall that the range of $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ for $0\\le x<2\\pi $ is given by\n\n$$\n\\operatorname{csc}⁡x\\in (-\\infty ,-1]\\cup [1,\\infty ).\n$$\n\nSo, the equation $\\operatorname{csc}⁡x=k$ has no solutions for $k\\in (-1,1).$ \n\nTherefore, of the given equations, only equation $4\\operatorname{csc}⁡x+1=\\operatorname{csc}⁡x$ has no solutions."} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186403237 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186124472 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "3bd13aa5-83a8-5b23-96cb-ea665039ea30" :knowledge-point/title "Identifying an Invertible Function From Its Graph"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "8ea56554-53cf-5bb5-92c1-60ae95348ed1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438374} {:answer/id #uuid "77d7988f-0bac-5a63-a670-9c28bfc5d65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438375}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "77d7988f-0bac-5a63-a670-9c28bfc5d65f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438375} :answer-field/id #uuid "b8217e6b-d9bd-526b-b658-c6c7f2dc6afe" :answer-field/key "![Graph I](images/d6/d6b08ae444dd7a18dc1d497e2ebcee35a3e44c34e44109484d4cc18be95d3629.svg)" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186408166 :db/ident :answer-field.type/checkbox} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186439216} {:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "92e341ec-14cf-5956-94d0-bfeb5c217021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "false" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438376} {:answer/id #uuid "03dea3b4-04b0-5566-a4b6-bd97af421174" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "true" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186438377}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "92e341ec-14cf-5956-94d0-bfeb5c217021" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045499 :db/ident :answer.